<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Same style as values/themes.xml plus the API 29+ force-dark opt-out.
         Styles do not merge across resource qualifiers, so the whole style is
         repeated here; keep the two definitions in sync.

         android:forceDarkAllowed=false is a REAL, measured fix, not paranoia.
         On a Mi Note 10 (MIUI 12, Android 11) with system dark mode on, HWUI
         force dark dimmed this app's every pixel to ~72% of its declared
         value: the Sign In button's fill rendered (175,45,67) instead of the
         declared rose (244,63,94), dropping the label's contrast from a
         computed 4.45:1 to a measured 2.81:1 - below the 3.0:1 floor for
         large text. Measured 2026-09-02 by screenshot pixel sampling:
           night off               -> fill (244,63,94)  exactly as declared
           night on, prop false    -> fill (195,50,75)  0.80x (MIUI smart dark)
           night on, prop true     -> fill (175,45,67)  0.72x (+ AOSP force dark)
         Two dimmers stack on MIUI - the OS's own "smart dark" and AOSP force
         dark via debug.hwui.force_dark - and both honour this attribute. An
         app that is ALREADY dark must say so, or the OS "helpfully" darkens
         it a second time and every computed contrast in the theme is off by
         a third on the exact phones this product sells to.

         Since the five-theme switch the app is no longer always dark - Day and
         Sand are light palettes - which makes this opt-out MORE load-bearing,
         not less: force dark would invert exactly those two, repainting a
         WCAG-solved light palette into something nobody measured. Opting out
         once, for every palette, is the only reading that holds for all five.

         android:windowBackground is the Calm-coloured PREVIEW-window fallback,
         not a value that matches every theme - see values/colors.xml. -->
    <style name="Theme.Aura" parent="Theme.AppCompat.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/window_background</item>
        <item name="android:forceDarkAllowed">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
